WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
Several kinds of warblers hunt insects in the same spruce forest.

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.960
How can they share one place without running out of food?

3
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.060
Watch the same tree as the birds choose where to search.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.080
Gold outlines the shared forest, while each bird keeps its own color.

5
00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:24.103
The spruce forest is their habitat, the place where they live.

6
00:00:25.363 --> 00:00:26.403
A niche is different.

7
00:00:27.623 --> 00:00:31.823
It is an organism's role: how it lives and uses resources there.

8
00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:34.323
Food is part of a niche.

9
00:00:35.543 --> 00:00:40.303
So are where and when it feeds, the space it uses, what eats it, and its ecosystem

10
00:00:40.303 --> 00:00:41.303
role.

11
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:46.247
A resource is something a living thing needs and takes from its habitat.

12
00:00:47.727 --> 00:00:50.367
Food, water, shelter, and space are resources.

13
00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:55.327
Each warbler searches spruce needles with its small, thin bill.

14
00:00:56.747 --> 00:01:00.367
Finding insects supplies food, and food helps the bird survive.

15
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:06.377
When organisms need the same resource and it is in short supply, they compete.

16
00:01:08.237 --> 00:01:11.357
Competition is a struggle for resources in short supply.

17
00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:16.237
If birds search the same branches, they may reach for the same insect.

18
00:01:16.237 --> 00:01:20.837
Their niches overlap where their resource use overlaps.

19
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.310
A species is one kind of living thing.

20
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.050
The warblers are different species, even though their bodies and food look similar.

21
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.190
Competition can leave one kind with enough food and another short.

22
00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.530
It becomes more likely when their shared resource is scarce.

23
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:46.770
Now predict. Insects become scarce, and every kind searches every branch.

24
00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.990
Will more birds get enough food, or will more fall short?

25
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.450
Keep your answer while the insect dots fade.

26
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:05.607
With scarce insects and every kind searching everywhere, more kinds are likely to

27
00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:07.187
fall short in this simple model.

28
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:10.667
Now let each color favor its own tree zone.

29
00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:15.147
The birds still share one forest, but fewer paths cross.

30
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:20.127
Different niches can reduce competition when insects are limited.

31
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:23.607
That can help more kinds share the habitat.

32
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.570
The model simplifies nature.

33
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.510
Real niches include much more than feeding zones.

34
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.330
Birds also change where they search when food or weather changes.

35
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.290
Different use does not remove all competition.

36
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.070
It changes the chances of meeting over a limited resource.

37
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.780
That gives a testable explanation for the opening puzzle.

38
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.540
Shared habitat does not mean identical niche.

39
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:59.100
The next step is evidence: watch where real warblers spent time, then ask what those

40
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.560
observations can support.
